#X text 18 736 >7) These comments are for comments numbered >4) \, >5) and >6) among the sections above. Things sort of go left to right and the patch started to get crowded as I experimented with different ways of selecting frequencies of [osc~] and ways of routing the numbers to the destinations. >4) shows the use of a horizontal slider in the range of 0 to 127 which can be typical for MIDI notes. But I had to change the numbers to integers because Pd allows for in between frequencies by using floats with [mtof].;
#X text 781 731 >8) Onto >5) -> This is where things were getting crowded and I wanted to eliminate crossing lines. I had seen [send] and [receive] objects before so that is how the mechanism at >5) terminates and is picked up again at the point that feeds into the [/~] before the [dac~]. Looking at the [/~] before the [dac~] there is a [r steps number] feeding into its cold inlet. This eliminates another line from the section that determines the tempo of the sequencer and specifically \, the number of steps in the sequencer. The # of steps can be varied from 4 to 8 - arbitrary numbers.;
#X text 1218 734 >9 Onto >6) -> These sections receive a couple of things. Just below >6) is the [r step_6_freq] which is actually step 7 in the sequencer. But the counter starts at 0 That is the same for all the steps \, their numbers in respective places being 1 less than their respective steps. Take note that for the Volume section near the top of the patch \, just to the right of the comment labeled >1) \, I used a [send volume]. [send] and [s] both work. There is a label which called an argument - which is the "volume" in [send volume]. Note that it saves space to use the abbreviation [s] \, which I also use.;
